  "transcript": "# Tourism\n\n## Why the overnight visitor levy is being proposed\n> Government wants local income and incentives for growth, but witnesses disagree on whether a levy is the right or fair tool.\n\nChair: Let’s start: what problem is the overnight visitor levy trying to solve, and will it work?\nBernard Donoghue: It tries to fix three things: squeezes on local government funds, the international precedent for city taxes, and a political push for devolution. If it’s carefully designed and the money is truly reinvested into tourism, it could help.\nAlistair Handyside: I agree with Bernard's diagnosis but worry about delivery: how it's collected, who decides spending, and the real local impacts—especially outside cities.\nAnthony Breach: The levy is part of fiscal devolution: the UK is unusually centralised. If mayors get revenue they’ll have better incentives to grow local economies. But the levy must be pro-growth.\n\n\n## Cities versus coasts: uneven effects\n> Big cities may absorb a levy and use it strategically; rural and coastal areas fear lost visitors and disproportionate harm.\n\nChair: Is there a clear economic case for the levy beyond politics?\nBernard Donoghue: Potentially. Tourism is the UK's fifth largest industry and a major employer. But policy is patchy—museum entry charges, VAT cuts for attractions and the levy were decided separately, not under a national tourism strategy.\nAlistair Handyside: Domestic rural and coastal tourism has fallen nearly 30% since 2022. A levy hits those markets hardest because domestic visitors are very price-sensitive.\nAnthony Breach: Evidence shows domestic tourism is more elastic than international travel. So coasts and smaller destinations risk losing visitors if prices rise; big cities less so.\nNigel Huddleston: If it goes ahead, what form should it take—flat charge or percentage?\nBernard Donoghue: A percentage rate is fairer. A flat per-night fee is regressive—hitting budget stays harder than luxury hotels.\nAlistair Handyside: A flat fee is worse for businesses with seasonal and low-priced rooms. A percentage tracks price and is less regressive.\n\n\n## Who pays, who collects, and administrative headaches\n> Collection, platform bookings, exemptions and timing create big practical problems—small providers fear extra cost and complexity.\n\nTom Rutland: Practically, who collects the levy when bookings are made through online platforms—the host or the platform?\nBernard Donoghue: No clarity yet. Different countries vary. Legislation must set legal responsibility; it needs advance notice because many stays are booked years ahead.\nAlistair Handyside: Operators will need time to build systems. Quarterly reporting is likely and burdensome. Small businesses might collect tiny sums but still bear admin costs.\nTom Rutland: Would guests pay on arrival?\nAlistair Handyside: That’s bad—disputes at check-in. The tax should appear in the booking price, but remittance must account for cancelled bookings.\nAlistair Handyside: Exemptions (children, disabled visitors, veterans) sound right but are administratively heavy. Scotland’s model forces providers to claim refunds and increases costs.\nNigel Huddleston: Will this be a business tax borne by businesses rather than visitors?\nAlistair Handyside: Yes. Many businesses won’t be able to pass the cost on; margins and investment suffer. It’s a business tax before profit.\n\n\n## Hypothecation and local accountability\n> Witnesses split: some want levy money ringfenced to tourism; others say hypothecation can be a smokescreen and prefer mayoral accountability and behaviour change.\n\nDr Huq: If a levy is introduced, how should the proceeds be used to benefit tourists, businesses and communities?\nAnthony Breach: I oppose strict hypothecation. Hard ringfencing rarely delivers the promised local outcomes; better to require mayors to publish plans and be accountable for actions that grow the visitor economy.\nBernard Donoghue: I disagree. Hypothecation can protect non-statutory services—culture, heritage, green spaces—that are often first to be cut. San Francisco is cited as a scheme that rings fences and registers accommodation.\nAlistair Handyside: If money isn’t ringfenced, councils may absorb it into other pressures or replace existing tourism budgets with levy income, so specificity in law is needed.\nAnthony Breach: A middle way: require strategic authorities to publish clear plans and commitments tied to any decision to levy, and avoid equalising away the revenue so areas keep the returns of the policy.\n\n\n## Destination management, funding gaps and the need for strategy\n> Structures for national-to-local tourism exist, but LVEPs and national agencies are underfunded; witnesses demand a funded five-year strategy and clearer accountability.\n\nJo Stevens: Do the many organisations—VisitBritain, VisitEngland, LVEPs, DMOs—have clear roles?\nLaura Citron: The destination-management structure is right, but it's underfunded. London & Partners acts as an LVEP, convention bureau and inward-investment agency; long-term funding matters.\nMerryn Kidd: LVEPs help small businesses join up, but many run on volunteers with no full-time staff. Small, targeted funds would let us employ admin and do trade readiness work.\nJoss Croft: The de Bois review set sensible layers; the problem is money. VisitBritain’s grant-in-aid has fallen in real terms since 2000. Many LVEPs lack staff; 22% say they may not survive five years.\nNigel Huddleston: What’s realistic funding? Should it be mostly commercial or public?\nLaura Citron: London & Partners mixes grant and paid business membership. Even so, most of the budget is grant. Private money helps but isn’t a full solution.\nJoss Croft: Tour operators already invest—about £30m marketing the UK—but they need trade-ready product. Fund LVEPs so they can prepare businesses to be sold internationally.\nChair: Any final priority ask?\nJoss Croft: Publish a funded five-year growth plan with named delivery bodies, clear accountability across Government, and multi-year funding for destination development.\nMerryn Kidd: Tourism is a growth opportunity—treat the LVEPs as essential infrastructure and fund them accordingly.\nLaura Citron: Governance is fixed; now fund the system so it can work long-term.\n\n\n## Infrastructure, dispersal from London and practical steps\n> To spread visitors beyond London and support smaller places, the sector needs better transport links, digital connectivity and product that's bookable internationally.\n\nAlison Hume: Coastal places are attractive but hard to get to; should transport be a priority in the strategy?\nJoss Croft: Absolutely. Visitors need means, money and motivation. Rail access, affordable fares, and simpler ticketing are crucial. Events and regional air links also help dispersal.\nMerryn Kidd: In Somerset & Exmoor our problems are last-mile and last-25-mile transport and poor mobile connectivity—Wells lacks a station and large areas have weak 4G/5G.\nLaura Citron: In London, transport projects like the Elizabeth line transformed access, boosted investment and made large events and conferences more viable. Similar local investments make a difference.\nJoss Croft: Digital bookability is vital. Much of the world uses intermediaries—tour operators and OTAs—so businesses must be trade-ready, integrated with distribution systems, and digitally connected.\nChair: Final wrap-up: anything else you want the Committee to note?\nBernard Donoghue: Be careful and consult. There have been 26 tourism Ministers in 29 years; we need corporate memory, cross-departmental coordination and time to get this right.\nAlistair Handyside: Mandate robust economic impact assessments and proper local consultation before any scheme proceeds, especially to protect small operators.\nAnthony Breach: This levy may be right in some places and wrong in others; give mayors the choice, but ensure rules avoid equalising away incentives.\n",
